 {"id": "DF-BOARDCTL-45", "title": "[P3] --repair-events can CREATE a duplicate event id (renumber target never checked against ids later in the file) and still reports 'duplicate ids: 0'", "status": "pending", "priority": "P3", "reasoning": "Dogfood run 25, HEAD 900b7b5 and released v0.1.11 (identical behaviour). Fixture events.jsonl with ids 10, 9, 11 in file order: line 2 (id 9) descends below 10, so the repair renumbers it to runningMax+1 = 11 — an id that line 3 already carries. The command then reports `duplicate ids: 0` (it counted duplicates in the INPUT only) and `validate` prints `[warn] events.jsonl line 3: duplicate event id 11 (also line 2)` with `RESULT: OK`. Net effect: the repair's own census claims a clean input and says nothing about the output, while the file now has two events sharing one id — any consumer keyed on event id (watchdogs, dedupe-by-id, cross-board sync) sees an ambiguous record, and the id that used to name one specific event now names two. Code: internal/board/repair_events.go:145-180 — renumber uses a left-to-right running max and never consults the ids that appear LATER in the file.", "created_at": "2026-10-23T11:23:17Z", "updated_at": "2026-10-23T11:23:17Z", "created_by": "dogfood-boardctl-run25", "acceptance_criteria": "1) Fixture ids 10,9,11 must repair to a file where no two events share an id (renumber to 12, or base the renumber on the maximum numeric id over the whole file) and the report's duplicate count is computed on the OUTPUT. 2) `boardctl validate` on the repaired fixture prints no 'duplicate event id' line. 3) A regression test asserts both the uniqueness of the output ids and the reported duplicate count for this fixture. 4) `go test ./...` exits 0.", "capability_tags": [], "complexity": 3, "depends_on": [], "foreman_note": null, "commit_hash": null, "guard_result": null, "ci_result": null, "worker_summary": null, "detail": {"fingerprint": "1e544bcf138451833b636a110ca84da12ef5b6cf848ede17d0df66651e2ccb5b"}}
